<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edo Bay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edo Bay, now Tokyo Bay, is the bay on which Edo stands. In 1853 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthew-c-perry">Commodore Matthew Perry</a>'s '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-ships">Black Ships</a>' entered it and demanded that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> open to American trade, exposing the military weakness of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> and setting in motion the events that led to its fall.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edo-bay.jpg" alt="Edo Bay" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Copernicus Sentinel-2, ESA · CC BY-SA 3.0 igo · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tokyo_Bay_by_Sentinel-2,_2018-10-30.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
